      close: 'Close', help_title: 'How to use',
      help_html: `<h4>Build the circuit</h4><ul>
<li>Choose a component in the list, then click / tap on the grid to place it. You can also drag it from the list onto the circuit.</li>
<li><b>R</b> rotates, <b>F</b> mirrors (also with the buttons over the circuit when an element is selected).</li><li>Potentiometer: drag its wiper arrow along the resistor (mouse or finger), use the slider in its panel, or the keys <b>[</b> and <b>]</b>; the circuit is solved while it moves.</li>
<li>Wires: drag from a component terminal, or use the <b>Wire</b> tool (drag, or tap point after point; tap the last point again or press Esc to finish). Dragging a wire corner or a junction dot moves that point with its wires.</li>
<li>Wires connect at their ends and where an end touches another wire (a dot is drawn). Crossing wires are not connected. A wire with a branch on it is cut in two at the branch, so that each piece can be moved or deleted on its own.</li>
<li>Edit values with a double-click / double-tap. Values accept prefixes: 4k7, 2.2M, 10m, 100u, 1n…</li>
<li>Every circuit needs a ground (⏚): node voltages are measured from it.</li></ul>
<h4>Results</h4><ul>
<li>The circuit is solved after every change. Choose what to show with V, I, P and Nodes over the circuit.</li>
<li>Every element shows its <b>reference directions</b>: + and − for V and an arrow for I. Select the element and tap them to choose your own; the values carry the sign (a negative value means the real polarity or direction is the opposite). In resistors, diodes, LEDs and switches the current arrow always enters through + (passive convention): flipping one flips both; only the sources can take the generator convention. Power: positive = absorbed, negative (green) = delivered.</li><li>Node voltages are measured from ground; name a node by selecting one of its wires. The <b>Results</b> tab checks KCL at each node and KVL around each loop with your references.</li>
<li>The <b>Results</b> tab lists every element and the power balance.</li>
<li><b>Live changes</b>: the main value of a component (R, V, I, β, V<sub>γ</sub>) has a slider under its box; dragging it solves the circuit at every step. <b>📌</b> on an I–V plot pins it over the circuit: it stays while you select and change other components, its axes stay put and Q moves on fixed axes (⟲ fits them again, ⇄ switches input / output of a transistor, the title bar moves it).</li></ul>
<h4>Thévenin &amp; R<sub>eq</sub></h4><ul>
<li>Select a two-terminal element and choose “Thévenin seen by this element”, or use the <b>Thévenin A–B</b> tool and tap two nodes.</li></ul>
<h4>Models</h4><ul>
<li>Diode: ideal (0 V when on), constant drop V<sub>γ</sub> with optional r<sub>d</sub>, or exact (Shockley: I = I<sub>S</sub>(e^{V/nV<sub>T</sub>} − 1)). Schottky: same models with lower V<sub>γ</sub> and higher I<sub>S</sub>.</li>
<li>Op-amp: ideal (infinite gain, no input current, zero output resistance), with optional saturation at ±Vsat.</li>
<li>Switch (no spring): the first click or tap selects it, like any component; once selected, each click opens or closes it, and it stays there (or use the switch button in the bar over the circuit). Push button (spring): select it, then it is closed while you hold it down (normally open, NO) or open while held (normally closed, NC), and it springs back when released. Ideal: closed = a wire (0 V), open = an open circuit (I = 0).</li>
<li>Transistor (BJT): simple model (cutoff, active with V<sub>BE</sub> = 0.7 V and I<sub>C</sub> = βI<sub>B</sub>, or saturation with V<sub>CE</sub> = V<sub>CE,sat</sub>) or real model (Ebers-Moll with the Early effect: exponential junctions, smooth saturation).</li></ul>
<h4>Rotate and mirror</h4><ul>
<li>Keyboard: <b>R</b> rotates and <b>F</b> mirrors the selection or the component being placed; with nothing selected, R takes a resistor; while a wire is being drawn, R flips its corner (horizontal first ↔ vertical first).</li></ul><h4>Moving</h4><ul><li>Drag a component (or a selection): the wires attached to it follow (rubber band) and become straight pieces again when you release. Turn it off with <b>🔗</b> in the bar over the circuit or in Settings to move components without their wires, for example to disconnect them; <b>Alt + drag</b> does the opposite of the current setting for one move.</li>
<li>Touch: select the component and use <b>⟳</b> / <b>⇋</b> in the bar over the circuit. While placing, the same bar rotates the component before you tap the grid.</li></ul>
<h4>Zoom and pan</h4><ul>
<li>Mouse: <b>wheel</b> = zoom around the pointer; drag with the <b>right or middle button</b>, or hold <b>Space</b> and drag = pan; drag on the background = box selection (the components inside and every wire it touches).</li>
<li>Touchpad: <b>two fingers</b> = pan, <b>pinch</b> = zoom.</li><li>Touch screen: <b>one finger</b> on the background = pan, <b>two fingers</b> = pinch zoom and pan; <b>two fingers held still, then moved up or down</b> = zoom; long press on the background, or a double tap and drag (slow is fine) = box selection, a quick double tap on an element = properties; one finger dragging at once = pan (also over a component); to move a component or a wire corner: long press, then drag.</li><li>While dragging something to the edge of the drawing, the view scrolls by itself.</li>
<li>Buttons + / − / ⤢ (fit the circuit) at the bottom right; keys <b>+</b>, <b>−</b>, <b>0</b>; <b>double click or double tap on the background</b> = fit the circuit.</li></ul><h4>Changing a value quickly</h4><ul><li>Select a component and turn the <b>mouse wheel over it</b> (or scroll with two fingers on a touchpad, or move two fingers up and down on it on a touch screen): resistance along the E12 series, sources in steps of a tenth of their decade, potentiometer position, β of a transistor. One Ctrl+Z undoes the whole series of steps.</li></ul>
<h4>Other keys</h4><ul><li>Components: <b>R</b> resistor, <b>V</b> voltage source, <b>I</b> current source, <b>G</b> ground, <b>A</b> op-amp, <b>D</b> diode, <b>T</b> transistor (NPN; T again: PNP), <b>S</b> switch (it appears under the pointer, ready to be dropped).</li><li>Del, Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Y, Ctrl+C / Ctrl+V, Ctrl+D, Ctrl+A, arrows (move the selection), W (wire), Enter (properties), Esc.</li></ul>`,
